[
    {
        "content": "<p>I was surprised that this </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Function</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Injective</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">λ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↦</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span>\n<span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">tauto</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>is not proven by <code>grind</code>. Is there some rule of thumb to understand when <code>tauto</code> works but <code>grind</code> doesn't?</p>",
        "id": 585050272,
        "sender_full_name": "Lauri Oksanen",
        "timestamp": 1776013218
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Probably the issue is that <code>tauto</code> by its nature is applying functions where possible, so it applies h to x and y to obtain a contradiction with x = y. <code>grind</code> must not automatically apply Function.injective, but I'm not sure that's the best solution, because it could lead to many applications in contexts where it isn't needed. Perhaps better would be to make a proof of the fact that \"the const function is injective iff the domain is a subsingleton\", and add this fact to grind.</p>",
        "id": 585053961,
        "sender_full_name": "Bolton Bailey",
        "timestamp": 1776016874
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If you'd like to see everywhere in Mathlib this happens, there is a report at: <a href=\"#narrow/channel/428973-nightly-testing/topic/nightly-testing.20regression.20log/with/584800835\">https://leanprover.zulipchat.com/#narrow/channel/428973-nightly-testing/topic/nightly-testing.20regression.20log/with/584800835</a></p>",
        "id": 585054354,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris Henson",
        "timestamp": 1776017191
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It does seem somehow like a bit of an issue that grind cannot conclude two terms of subsingleton type are equal, but I am having trouble making a grind annotation to make it work</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Subsingleton</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">grind</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- failed</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 585054678,
        "sender_full_name": "Bolton Bailey",
        "timestamp": 1776017444
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks. Note that also <code>tauto</code> fails in the subsingleton example.</p>",
